entrances  are blocked and surveillance is total as  we haven't been able to get much closer  but there are still faint traces to be  found  potential evidence for what critics say  is cultural genocide something  china denies there's a few more of them  than there are of us so  we're stopping here this is a place  where the dead are paved over  and the living are equally silent  fled china with her family in 2016. they  were from hotan  and her grandfather was buried in a  cemetery there  is a  [Music]  she asked us to see what had happened to  the cemetery  [Music]  some of it is obvious a vast recently  expanded prison complex  visible from the air as we flew over  xinjiang  and in hotan there is the predictable  government escort  up to five cars followed us during our  visit  tawazim cemetery where murghaba's  grandfather was buried  to begin with we weren't sure we even  had the right place  there was simply nothing here so  megabet talked about this cemetery as  being full of tombs ornaments  all sorts of decorations people coming  here and bringing flowers  so very busy place and you look at it  now  there's none of that left it's all been  completely erased  but i was wrong we just had to look more  closely  pay more attention we found the wrecked  wooden structure  of a grave marking pummeled into the  earth  this is a building where bodies would be  wrapped in white linen before burial  apparently shuttered and then this  the man who said he owned the plot  stopped us from going further i  oh yeah look at that that is actually a  graveyard in the ground bleach white  a bone six different experts who looked  at this image have told us it is most  likely a human bone  the thigh bone of a child  but these were the only traces we could  find if the cemetery that once stood  here  on the other side a park has been built  with a monument proclaiming ethnic unity  but satellite images show what was here  before and how it has changed  in 2018 there were hundreds of graves  then in 2019 they started disappearing  until none were left  we shared what we had found with murga  killer  the cemetery is not a one-off but part  of a program  we worked with experts to understand  satellite images of the city  the changes they show are stark another  cemetery  much bigger called sultanim has been  paved over  and turned into a car park the tall  islamic style tower on the town bazaar  in the centre of the city  has been taken down and a mosque has  also been bulldozed  the site is now in use again converted  into another car park  on the ground the effect is even more  severe this  was what the hectare mosque looked like  and this  is now the destruction of sultan and  cemetery is even more vivid  a void carved out of the old heart of  the city chinese state media has said  that the cemetery has been moved to the  outskirts of town  this is what's left of sultanum cemetery  and this is as close as we can get as  you can see  it's a huge construction site we can't  get down to where  the graves used to be but you can see  the same buildings in the background  here and they're digging  very deep foundations the rest of the  cemetery over here has been turned  into a car park for a nearby hospital  this was once a very very important  place and simply there is no trace left  on religious sites are framed as  a way to reduce uh  extremism uh despite the fact that  these sites tend to be sort of very  ordinary parts of the  local community and and uh local  tradition  but taken all together what it looks  like is an attempt to  completely re-engineer uyghur culture  there was one more religious site we  wanted to visit a shrine in the desert  that usually at this time of year  attracts hundreds of pilgrims  we found multiple entryways blocked  we've got our three friends watching us  we haven't been able to get much closer  so we're just going to go about  two and a half kilometers on foot and  see if we can get to this shrine  the shrine is also a tomb of one of the  earliest muslims to arrive in the hoten  region  a symbolic ancestor of the uyga people  even in the desert though we were not  alone  we were being tracked but we pressed on  at this stage we're about 200 meters  from the shrine  the chinese government says it welcomes  journalists  so much for there's a few more of them  than there are  of us so we're stopping here going back  another two kilometers  we don't know we're already away very  much eating daikon  then wait a minute  we asked the chinese government for a  response to our questions but did not  receive a reply ahead of broadcast  at a news conference in march in beijing  a spokesperson for the xinjiang  government said  the constitution of the people's  republic of china stipulates that  chinese citizens enjoy the rights to  have religious belief  no government organizations social  groups or individuals  shall force them to have or not to have  religious beliefs  not how they discriminate against people  having or not having religious beliefs  all legitimate religious activities are  protected by the country  for uighurs themselves it is simply  another blow upon blow  [Music]  memory for now still endures but it is  under attack  in xinjiang tom sky news hotel

